Thanks Mike! This is useful although I also feel it is a bit hacky. I agree with you that mixed-effects already give me what I want. However, one issue is that many times, depending on the model structure and the amount of data available, lmer() doesn't converge and/or pvals.fnc() doesn't work if the model contains random correlation terms. So I keep going back to anovas because they are more robust and they are always a good sanity check. I'll try your suggestion. I also think it should be possible to adapt boot.ci <http://boot.ci>() to work on the output of ezBoot, which would be less hacky!

